# open62541-qnx change: tcp-recv-eof
# SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0
#
# title: Take recv() == 0 as the end of the connection, whatever errno says
# file: open62541.c
# upstream: arch/eventloop_posix_tcp.c
# test: robust.peer_close
#
# recv() returns 0 when the peer has closed the connection, and it leaves
# errno alone. The code looks at errno also in that case. If errno still holds
# EAGAIN from an earlier call, the closed connection is taken for a temporary
# condition: it stays registered, it stays readable, and the event loop spins
# on it at full CPU load.
#
# An earlier EAGAIN is the normal case on QNX after a message that did not fit
# into the socket buffer at once: accepted sockets inherit O_NONBLOCK from the
# listen socket there (they do not on Linux), so send() returns EAGAIN and the
# stack waits and retries, successfully, without touching errno again.
#
# The same holds for a client on any system: its socket is made non-blocking
# explicitly, so a large request can leave EAGAIN behind, and a server that
# closes the connection afterwards goes unnoticed.
#
@@ hunk
